# FlowDeploy — Orchestrator (Architect → Developer → Senior Reviewer)

> **Purpose**: every user request that modifies files in this repository MUST
> flow through three phases with explicit visual banners and a mandatory
> human approval gate between Architect and Developer. This rule orchestrates
> the existing `flowdeploy-*.mdc` rules — it does **not** replace them.
>
> Subordinate to [`flowdeploy-development-protocol.mdc`](./flowdeploy-development-protocol.mdc).
> Reuses [`flowdeploy-backend-go.mdc`](./flowdeploy-backend-go.mdc),
> [`flowdeploy-agent-go.mdc`](./flowdeploy-agent-go.mdc),
> [`flowdeploy-frontend-react.mdc`](./flowdeploy-frontend-react.mdc),
> [`flowdeploy-proto-grpc.mdc`](./flowdeploy-proto-grpc.mdc),
> [`flowdeploy-shared-go.mdc`](./flowdeploy-shared-go.mdc),
> [`flowdeploy-security.mdc`](./flowdeploy-security.mdc),
> [`flowdeploy-ops.mdc`](./flowdeploy-ops.mdc).

---

## 1. When this rule fires

**MUST run**: every request that implies creating, editing, renaming, moving
or deleting any file tracked by this repository — including source code,
migrations, protos, scripts, workspace rules, docs, configuration, CI
workflows, `.env.example`, `Makefile`, `package.json`, `go.mod`, etc.

**MUST NOT run** (answer directly, no pipeline):

- Pure Q&A about the codebase ("where is X defined?", "what does Y do?").
- Exploration, search and read-only tool usage.
- Clarifying questions sent back to the user.
- Tasks the user has scoped as `--quick`, `--hotfix` or `--skip-pipeline`
  in the same message (still must log a one-line note explaining why).

When in doubt, assume it fires.

---

## 2. Visual feedback contract

The assistant MUST print a **banner block** at the start of each phase. The
banner is a fenced code block (triple backticks, no language tag) so the
chat renders it in a monospaced font with visible contrast. Missing or
silently skipping a banner is a protocol violation.

### 2.1 Banner block shape

Every banner block has 5 lines, in this exact order:

1. Phase header — `<emoji> [PHASE NAME] — <short subtitle>`
2. `rule    · <comma-separated list of governing .mdc files>`
3. `skill   · <skill name or n/a>`
4. `agent   · <subagent_type or n/a>`
5. `context · <one-sentence statement of what this phase will do in THIS request>`

Formatting rules:

- Columns are aligned with 4 spaces between the label and the `·`. Keep
  the separator (middle-dot) consistent.
- `rule` MUST list every `.mdc` file that governs the phase. The
  orchestrator itself (`flowdeploy-orchestrator.mdc`) is implicit and
  omitted from every phase except Architect.
- `skill` lists one or more skill slugs (`senior-code-reviewer`,
  `application-security-expert`, etc.). Use `n/a` when no skill applies.
- `agent` lists the subagent_type from the Task tool (`explore`,
  `generalPurpose`, `shell`, `browser-use`, etc.) OR `n/a` if the
  assistant does the work itself without delegating to a subagent.
- `context` is a single sentence (≤ 140 chars) tailored to the current
  request — never a generic placeholder.
- One banner per phase entry. If the Developer touches multiple areas,
  emit one full banner block per area (Backend, Frontend, Proto, ...).

### 2.2 Worked example (Architect)

```
🏛️ [ARCHITECT] — analysis & architecture
rule    · flowdeploy-orchestrator.mdc, flowdeploy-development-protocol.mdc
skill   · software-architecture-expert
agent   · explore (readonly)
context · Analyzing impacted files and drafting design + risks before any edit.
```

