## 3. Phase 1 — Architect (🏛️)

Goal: produce a short, high-signal architectural plan and a risk review
**before** any file is edited.

Mandatory outputs (in this order, under the banner):

1. **Understanding** — 1–3 sentences summarizing what the user asked.
2. **Impacted surface** — bullet list of files/packages/layers that will be
   touched. Include generated code and migrations.
3. **Proposed design** — concrete approach. For backend: which service,
   repository, SQL, gRPC RPC. For frontend: which feature folder, hook,
   component, route. For proto: which message/RPC. For infra: which
   container/compose entry.
4. **Trade-offs & alternatives** — at least one alternative considered,
   with a one-line reason for rejection.
5. **Risk & security check** — map to `flowdeploy-security.mdc`: auth,
   mTLS, secrets, webhook signature, SQL injection, `sh -c`. State
   "no impact" explicitly when that is the case.
6. **Tests plan** — what tests will be written or updated, in which
   packages. Follow the Non-Negotiable #6 from the development protocol.
7. **Out of scope** — what the assistant will explicitly NOT do in this
   change, even if tempting.

Hard limits:

- No file edits during Architect.
- May use read-only tools (`Read`, `Grep`, `Glob`, `Shell` with non-mutating
  commands) to confirm assumptions.
- Keep the whole Architect block under ~60 lines. If you need more, you
  are probably planning two changes — split them.

---

## 4. Phase 2 — Approval gate (✅)

After the Architect block, the assistant MUST call the `AskQuestion` tool
with exactly this shape:

- One question, id = `architect_approval`.
- Prompt = "Aprova o plano do Architect acima para prosseguir para Developer?".
- Options (single-select):
  - `approve` — "Aprovado — prossiga para Developer"
  - `adjust` — "Ajustar o plano — envio os ajustes no pr\u00f3ximo turno"
  - `cancel` — "Cancelar — n\u00e3o fa\u00e7a nada"

Gate behavior:

| Answer    | Next step                                                                 |
| --------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------------- |
| `approve` | Proceed to Developer                                                      |
| `adjust`  | Stop, wait for the user's next message with the adjustments               |
| `cancel`  | Print the `🛑 [PIPELINE CANCELLED]` banner block and STOP. Do not edit any file. |

Hard rules:

- NO file modification before `approve`.
- If the user skips the question and just sends a follow-up message, treat
  it as `adjust` and re-run Architect on the new input.
- If the change is trivially small (single-line typo fix, single-file
  rename with no public API change), the assistant MAY inline the Architect
  block and still use the approval gate — the gate is never skipped.

---

## 5. Phase 3 — Developer (🔨 / 🎨 / 🧩 / 🔧 / 📦 / 🧱 / 📚)

Only enters after `approve`. The Developer phase segments work by area and
delegates each area to the matching rule:

| Area       | Banner                          | Rule of record                                |
| ---------- | ------------------------------- | --------------------------------------------- |
| Backend    | `🔨 [DEVELOPER / BACKEND]`      | `flowdeploy-backend-go.mdc`                   |
| Agent      | `🔧 [DEVELOPER / AGENT]`        | `flowdeploy-agent-go.mdc`                     |
| Frontend   | `🎨 [DEVELOPER / FRONTEND]`     | `flowdeploy-frontend-react.mdc`               |
| Proto      | `🧩 [DEVELOPER / PROTO]`        | `flowdeploy-proto-grpc.mdc`                   |
| Shared Go  | `📦 [DEVELOPER / SHARED]`       | `flowdeploy-shared-go.mdc`                    |
| Infra / CI | `🧱 [DEVELOPER / INFRA]`        | `flowdeploy-ops.mdc`                          |
| Docs       | `📚 [DEVELOPER / DOCS]`         | `flowdeploy-development-protocol.mdc` §12     |

Ordering rules:

1. **Proto first**: if the change touches `apps/proto/**`, do Proto before
   Backend and Agent. Regenerate via `make proto` as part of this sub-phase.
2. **Backend before Frontend**: API contract (handlers + response envelope)
   is finalized before the frontend is wired.
3. **Tests in the same sub-phase**: do not defer tests to a separate phase.
   The test edit MUST appear under the same banner as the code it exercises.
4. **Migrations co-located with Backend**: SQL migrations and the matching
   repository code land under the Backend banner.

Every sub-phase MUST:

- Print its banner once, before the first edit in that area.
- End with a 2–4 line "delta" summary: files touched + intent.
- Honour every "Forbidden Practices" item of the development protocol.

Hard stop conditions (the Developer MUST halt and raise to the user):

- Plan drift: the work no longer matches what the Architect approved.
- A previously unseen security-sensitive surface appears (auth, mTLS,
  secrets, webhook signature, SQL composition).
- A migration needs to be irreversible or destructive (`DROP COLUMN`,
  `TRUNCATE`, data migration that cannot be rolled back).

---

## 6. Phase 4 — Senior Review (🔍)

After the last Developer sub-phase, print the Senior banner and run the
7-point mandatory checklist below. Each item is PASS / FAIL / N/A with a
one-line justification.

1. **Rules compliance** — re-read the rule that governs each touched area
   (backend, agent, frontend, proto, shared, security, ops) and confirm
   the change respects the "Forbidden" and "Conventions" sections.
2. **Security** — re-run the section 1–15 checklist of
   `flowdeploy-security.mdc` for every secret/auth/mTLS/webhook/shell/SQL
   surface the change touches.
3. **Self code review** — identify at least one concrete improvement
   (naming, error wrapping, cognitive complexity, duplication) or state
   "none found, pass".
4. **Tests** — list which tests were added/updated and the command to run
   them locally (`go test ./internal/engine/...`,
   `pnpm --filter @paasdeploy/frontend run test`, etc.).
5. **Lints & typecheck** — list the exact commands the user should run:
   `pnpm run lint:go-quick`, `pnpm --filter @paasdeploy/frontend run lint`,
   `pnpm --filter @paasdeploy/frontend run typecheck`.
6. **Build** — list the build commands affected:
   `cd apps/backend && pnpm run build`, `pnpm --filter @paasdeploy/frontend run build`,
   `cd apps/agent && go build ./cmd/agent`.
7. **Cross-references** — verify consistency between touched code and
   `README.md`, `CHANGELOG.md`, `AGENTS.md`, `docs/**`. If any is stale,
   list the follow-up as an open item.

If any check is FAIL, the Senior Reviewer MUST state it plainly, propose
a concrete fix, apply it, and re-run the affected checks. Do not close
the pipeline with a FAIL.

Pipeline closure banner (MUST appear at the very end, same 5-line shape):

```
✅ [PIPELINE COMPLETE] — all checks PASS
rule    · <union of all rules touched during Developer + Senior Review>
skill   · senior-code-reviewer, principal-engineering-workflow
agent   · n/a
context · Architect plan "<one-liner>" delivered across <areas> with all 7 checks PASS.
```

followed by:

```
• Architect approved: <one-liner>
• Developer areas: <list>
• Senior Review: all 7 checks PASS (or list of PASS with N/A)
• Suggested next steps: <0–3 bullets>
```

If the Senior raises unresolved items, use the same banner shape but with
the warning header and list the open items:

```
⚠️ [PIPELINE COMPLETE WITH OPEN ITEMS]
rule    · <union of all rules touched>
skill   · senior-code-reviewer, principal-engineering-workflow
agent   · n/a
context · <N> open items require follow-up; ranked in the list below.
```

---

## 7. Escape hatches

- `--quick` in the user message → skip Architect narrative; still print
  the banners, still use the approval gate with a condensed 3-bullet
  Architect block, still run Senior Review.
- `--hotfix` → allowed for production-critical 1-file fixes; Architect
  block may be 3 lines; approval gate still required; Senior Review
  still mandatory and security check is non-negotiable.
- `--skip-pipeline` → only honoured when the user clearly states a
  non-code task (docs tweak, renaming an untracked file). The assistant
  MUST print a `⏭️ [PIPELINE SKIPPED]` banner block (same 5-line shape:
  rule, skill, agent, context) explaining why the pipeline is bypassed.

Banner shapes for escape / abort states (same 5-line shape):

```
🛑 [PIPELINE CANCELLED]
rule    · flowdeploy-orchestrator.mdc
skill   · n/a
agent   · n/a
context · User chose `cancel` at the approval gate. No files were modified.
```

```
⏭️ [PIPELINE SKIPPED]
rule    · flowdeploy-orchestrator.mdc
skill   · n/a
agent   · n/a
context · <reason stated by the user or inferred from the request>.
```

Abuse of escape hatches defeats the orchestrator. If the request feels
like an escape-hatch attempt but clearly touches backend/agent/proto/pki
code, the assistant MUST refuse and run the full pipeline.

---

## 8. Interaction with the existing protocol

This rule sits on top of `flowdeploy-development-protocol.mdc`:

- The protocol defines WHAT is allowed/forbidden (Sections 2, 4, 5, 6, 7).
- This orchestrator defines HOW every change is shaped (phases, banners,
  gate, reviewer).
- In any conflict, the protocol wins; the orchestrator adjusts its phase
  output to respect it.

No other rule may override the orchestrator's banners or the approval
gate. Rules may, however, add mandatory sub-checks inside a phase (for
example `flowdeploy-security.mdc` tightens the Senior Review).

---

**End of FlowDeploy Orchestrator.**
